The solvothermal reaction between \(\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BPV}\) in the presence of dimethylformamide (DMF) and trifluoroacetic acid (TFA) at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) afforded BPV-MOF with a UiO framework of \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{BPV})_{6}\) in \(40\%\) yield. In contrast, mBPV-MOF was synthesized in \(40\%\) yield by heating \(\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}\) with \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BPV}\) and \(4{,}4^{\circ}\)-bis(carboxyethenyl)-1,1'-biphenyl \(\mathrm{(H_{2}B P H V)}\) (in a 1:2 molar ratio) in the presence of DMF and TFA at \(100\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Similarly,
mPT-MOF was synthesized in \(45\%\) yield by heating \(\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}\) and a mixture of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PT}\) and \(4{,}4^{\circ}\)-bis(carboxyphenyl)-2-nitro-1,1'-biphenyl \(\mathrm{(H_{2}T P H N)}\) in 1:2 molar ratio in a DMF solution in the presence of TFA at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The presence of bipyridyl- and phenanthryl-containing dicarboxylate linkers in mBPV-MOF and mPT-MOF, respectively, was established and quantified by taking \({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) NMR spectra of the digested MOFs. NMR studies consistently revealed that the ratio of biphenyl and bipyridine in the mBPV-MOF or the ratio of tetraphenyl and phenanthroline in mPT-MOF is approximately 2:1, consistent with the molar ratio in the feed (Figure S6 and S10, Supporting Information [SI]). Nitrogen sorption measurements indicate that both mBPV-MOF and mPT-MOF are highly porous with a BET surface area of \(1207\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) and 3834 \(\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) respectively (Figure S8 and S12, SI), and pore sizes of \(7\mathrm{~\AA~}\) and \(7.7\mathrm{~\AA~}\) respectively (Figure S18 and S21, SI).
The structures of BPV-MOF and mBPV-MOF were established by comparing the powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of the MOFs with the predicted pattern from a single crystal structure of BPHV-MOF which was synthesized from \(\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}\) and \(4{,}4^{\circ}\)-bis(carboxyethenyl)-1,1'-biphenyl \(\mathrm{(H_{2}B P H V)}\) linker in the presence of DMF and TFA at \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Figure 1). A single-crystal X-ray diffraction study revealed that BPHV-MOF adopts the UiO structure (Figure 1a), with the \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\upmu_{3}-\mathrm{O})_{4}(\upmu_{3}-\mathrm{OH})_{4}\) SBUs connected by the BPHV bridging linkers to afford the 12-connected fcu topology. However, BPHV-MOF crystallizes in a lower symmetry space group of \(I\overline{{4}}\) due to the bending nature of the BPHV linker. The broadening of (101) peak and appearance of several other peaks suggest structural distortion in the powder samples, which has been observed in other nanoscale MOFs. In the present case, the bent nature of the BPHV and BPV linkers provides additional mechanisms for structural distortions from the single crystal structure. Similarly, the structure of mPT-MOF was established by comparing the PXRD patterns with the simulated pattern from a single crystal structure of TPHN-MOF which was synthesized from \(\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}\) and \(4{,}4^{\circ}\)-bis(carboxyphenyl)-2-nitro-1,1'-biphenyl \(\mathrm{(H_{2}T P H N)}\) under similar conditions. TPHN-MOF adopts a typical UiO structure and crystallizes in the cubic \(F m\overline{{3}}\mathrm{m}\) space group (Figure 1).
